.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 143 AM PDT Sun Oct 18 2026
(Today and tonight)

Sunday and Monday mark the transition towards cooler conditions and
breezy onshore winds. Weakening high pressure allows the marine
layer to gradually reform and deepen, though it will be short-lived.
Nonetheless, any marine layer presence aids to initiate the
beginning of a cooling trend, even if it remains shallow and
confined to the coast Sunday morning. As the upper-level trough digs
southward along the West coast on Sunday, a strengthening onshore
pressure gradient will support moderate westerly breezes. This set
up can support wind gusts around 20-25 mph through the Petaluma
Gap/Valley with the Altamont and Patterson Passes experiencing gusts
closer to 25-30 mph.

Sunday maximum temperatures are forecast to reach the 60s to low 70s
along the coast, mid 70s to mid 80s across the interior, with the
usual hot spots reaching into the low 90s. On Monday, the maximum
temperatures drop further due to increased cold air advection from
the upper-level trough. Max temperatures are forecast to reach the
mid 50s to mid 60s at the coast, mid 60s to low 70s across the
interior, with the southernmost extent of the Central Coast
remaining in the mid 70s to low 80s.

