.LONG TERM...
Issued at 143 AM PDT Sun Oct 18 2026
(Monday through Saturday)

Monday night has a better chance for a marine layer around 1500 ft
as the center of the upper-level trough deepens along our coastline.
By Tuesday morning however, the first round of light rain arrives
with precipitation chances around 20-40% across the Bay Area and
Central Coast. Rainfall can overpower the marine layer by saturating
the atmosphere above it, thereby mixing out. Our stratus marine
layer is typically capped by an inversion, defined as layer of the
atmosphere where temperature increases with height. Introducing rain
into the mix perturbs and cools the inversion layer causing it to
disappear resulting in no marine layer. Though there will still be
deep convective cumulus clouds present!

By Tuesday afternoon, the cut-off low pressure system slows down and
stalls directly to our west with its center approximately 300-350
miles west of San Francisco. As it stalls, a plume of moisture
originating in the Eastern Pacific gets funneled into the eastern
portion of the system from the south. By Wednesday morning, the
system begins to propagate eastward towards the Central Coast. Given
the nature of cut-off lows combined with an increased moisture flux
and terrain interactions, there is potential for precipitation to
wrap around the system`s center giving way for widespread light rain
on Wednesday across the Bay Area and Central Coast (40-75% chance).
European and GFS ensemble members continue to agree on precipitable
water values above the 75th percentile on Wednesday. Rainfall
chances gradually diminish by Thursday, but cooler conditions remain
through the end of the week.

